--
Three fixes to the USB streaming path, in order of importance.

1. transfers and transfer_buffers are std::vectors that nothing ever sized,
   yet streaming_open_async() assigns through transfer_buffers[i] and
   transfers[i]. operator[] on an empty vector is UB -- data() is null, so the
   first assignment writes through a null pointer and the process dies inside
   streaming_open_async(). THIS is the crash: immediate SIGSEGV at stream
   start, at every sample rate, in both raw ADC and IQ mode, with nothing on
   stderr, while enumerate/open/configure/probe all work because they never
   touch these vectors.

2. libusb_dev_mem_alloc() had no fallback on Linux (macOS and Windows get
   malloc()). It is a zerocopy DMA optimisation -- an USBDEVFS_ALLOC_MEMORY
   mmap on usbfs -- that is unavailable in plenty of ordinary environments and
   returns NULL there. Now falls back to malloc() and records which allocator
   was used, so streaming_close() and the error unwind free correctly; calling
   libusb_dev_mem_free() on a malloc'd pointer would be its own bug.

3. FX3handler::StartStream() discarded streaming_open_async()'s return value
   and called streaming_start() regardless, which the author had documented
   in place: "// FIXME: Will crash if streaming hasn't properly started".
   It now reports and stays stopped, so any future open failure surfaces as a
   message instead of a crash.

Verified on an RX888 mk2 over SuperSpeed USB3 on Talos: before, sddc-cli
listen segfaulted in both modes; usbcore.usbfs_memory_mb was 1000 and the
kernel logged no USB errors, confirming a userspace fault.

Worth reporting upstream: renardspark/SDDC_Driver.
